# YAGI PROMPTS — environment (PLAN §7). Repo/package codename stays `playbook`.
# Copy to .env.local for development. Never commit a filled-in copy.

# --- Runtime mode (M7) --------------------------------------------------------
# The ONE switch that decides whether this process talks to the cloud.
#
#   local   PGlite (durable dir, else the .pglite snapshot) + .storage/ + no public bucket URLs,
#           REGARDLESS of DATABASE_URL / NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL being set below.
#   cloud   pg against DATABASE_URL + the Supabase storage driver + public bucket URLs.
#   unset   inferred: DATABASE_URL present => cloud, absent => local.
#
# Leave it unset here. The offline flows set it themselves (`npm run db:local`, `dev:free|pro|admin`,
# `build:local`, `start:local`, `sweep:anon`, CI); a deployment sets it to `cloud` explicitly.
# See docs/SETUP.md §0 for the full precedence table.
# PLAYBOOK_MODE=

# --- Supabase -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Project settings → API. The anon key is public by design; RLS is deny-all with no policies,
# so the browser client can do nothing but auth.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://YOUR-PROJECT.supabase.co
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=YOUR-ANON-KEY

# SERVER ONLY. lib/supabase/data-client.ts is guarded by `server-only`; never expose this.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=YOUR-SERVICE-ROLE-KEY

# Direct Postgres URI — used by `npm run seed` and by migration tooling. Server only.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres:PASSWORD@db.YOUR-PROJECT.supabase.co:5432/postgres

# --- App ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# The deployment's absolute origin. Read by the sitemap, robots.txt and every link inside a
# notification email (an inbox has no origin to resolve a relative path against). PLAN §7 names it
# APP_URL; both are accepted, NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL first.
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=http://localhost:3000
# APP_URL=

# --- Local development database -----------------------------------------------
# In LOCAL mode, lib/db/connect.ts loads this snapshot into an in-memory PGlite so the
# landing/pricing catalog has real seeded data. Build it with `npm run db:local`.
# PLAYBOOK_LOCAL_DB=.pglite/catalog.tar.gz
#
# A DURABLE local database (M5): writes survive the process, which the notification worker needs
# because it runs in a different one from `next dev`. Build it with
#   npm run db:local -- --dir .pglite/dev
# One process at a time — PGlite locks the directory. Preferred over the snapshot when set.
# PLAYBOOK_LOCAL_DB_DIR=.pglite/dev

# --- Content slots (docs/CONTENT-SLOTS.md) ------------------------------------
# Hero reel, rendered offline with HyperFrames. Unset ⇒ the in-code poster composition renders.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_HERO_REEL_SRC=
# NEXT_PUBLIC_HERO_REEL_TYPE=video/mp4
# NEXT_PUBLIC_HERO_REEL_POSTER=

# --- Media gateway (M2) -------------------------------------------------------
# Signs the ≤60s capability tokens the gateway hands out for private published objects
# (lib/media/token.ts). Unset ⇒ SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY, then a per-process random with a warning.
# Set this in production so tokens survive a restart. Any long random string.
# MEDIA_TOKEN_SECRET=

# --- Storage ------------------------------------------------------------------
# The driver follows PLAYBOOK_MODE above: local => .storage/, cloud => Supabase buckets.
#
# STORAGE_DRIVER is an ESCAPE HATCH that overrides the mode for storage only (cloud DB + local
# bytes, or the reverse). Left commented ON PURPOSE: an uncommented `local` here, copied into a
# deployment's env, would silently pin production to a filesystem that does not exist there.
# STORAGE_DRIVER=local
STORAGE_LOCAL_ROOT=.storage

# --- Stripe (M3) --------------------------------------------------------------
# All three are required together. With any of them missing (or left as these placeholders)
# `isBillingConfigured()` is false: checkout/portal answer a designed 503 and the CTAs render the
# "billing is not connected" panel instead of a dead button. Nothing crashes, nothing grants Pro.
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=sk_test_...
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=whsec_...
STRIPE_PRICE_PRO=price_...

# --- Local render-verify (M3, DEV ONLY) ---------------------------------------
# Both are ignored when NODE_ENV=production — `next build` inlines the check, so they are dead code
# in a production bundle and `npm start` can never see them. Set by `scripts/dev-as.ts`; you should
# not need to write them by hand.
#
# PLAYBOOK_DEV_IDENTITY grants an IDENTITY (a profiles.id that must already exist), never a tier:
# the tier still comes from that profile's real `subscriptions` row. Use `npm run dev:pro`.
# PLAYBOOK_DEV_IDENTITY=f0000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000002
#
# PLAYBOOK_FAKE_BILLING=1 swaps in the deterministic fake Stripe gateway so lock → checkout →
# unlock is walkable with no Stripe account. A real STRIPE_SECRET_KEY always wins.
# PLAYBOOK_FAKE_BILLING=1

# --- Email / notifications (M5) -----------------------------------------------
# Both are required together. With either missing, `getMailer()` is null: the worker claims nothing,
# prints "mailer NOT CONFIGURED" and changes no state. It never marks a delivery sent that no
# provider accepted.
RESEND_API_KEY=re_...
NOTIFY_FROM_EMAIL=YAGI PROMPTS <prompts@YOUR-DOMAIN>

# Signs the one-click unsubscribe links (lib/notify/unsubscribe.ts). They live in people's inboxes
# for months and have NO expiry, so this must be stable: unset ⇒ SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY, then a
# per-process random with a warning, and every link dies on the next restart. Any long random string.
# It is deliberately NOT the media secret — that one is a 60s read capability, this one is a write.
# NOTIFY_TOKEN_SECRET=

# DEV ONLY, and ignored when NODE_ENV=production (`next build` inlines the check, so it is dead code
# in a production bundle). Swaps in the deterministic mailer so the whole publish → expand → claim →
# send loop is runnable with no Resend account. A real RESEND_API_KEY always wins.
# PLAYBOOK_FAKE_MAILER=1
